page();h('5. Data-flow diagram');doc.add_picture(str(P/'D0_Data_Flow.png'),width=Inches(7.15))
p('Flow A - Speech-driven navigation. C1 receives microphone frames in memory through I1 and sends the utterance through I2. C2 uses I3 to obtain a transcript and structured constraint, validates it, and returns it to C1. C1 submits the proposed constraint plus current trip context through I4. C3 obtains place/route records through I5 and relevant weather records through I6, then returns a route proposal. C1 speaks its summary, receives accept/reject intent through I2 only after explicit spoken approval, updates the live route, and renders map guidance plus spoken directions through I1. For rejection, C1 requests another candidate through I4; no route update occurs.')
p('Flow B - Mute/privacy control. A passenger tap or locally recognized stop-listening command becomes a MuteEvent. C1 immediately blocks further capture/transmission, discards pending audio, cancels in-flight interpretation, and requests microphone shutdown through I1. It shows muted only when shutdown is confirmed; otherwise it force-stops capture and shows an error. Unmute requires a tap because a stopped mic cannot hear an unmute command. Remote interpretation is not a prerequisite for mute.')
p('Usage side flow. Each AI, Maps, or weather request attempt produces an I7/I8 UsageEvent. C4 aggregates counters into UsageSummary records exposed through I9. No audio, transcript, destination, or route geometry crosses these interfaces. Constraint and active-route state remain trip-scoped and are cleared at trip end; minimal usage counts may persist under a retention policy the team must set.')
page();doc.add_heading('Provider usage reporting',2);doc.add_picture(str(P/'D0_Usage_Flow.png'),width=Inches(7.15));p('C4 receives AI and Maps/weather usage events through I7 and I8. An authenticated developer queries I9; C4 returns per-trip UsageSummary records. The response flow is drawn explicitly above.');
page();h('5. Data-flow diagram continued')
table(['Acceptance criterion','Budget and required behavior','Responsible boundary'],[['AC-01.1','Detour <= 600 s; spoken proposal <= 15 words; explicit approval required; route update completes <= 3 s after the driver finishes saying "okay."','C1 -> C2 -> C3 -> C1, including providers'],['AC-01.2','0 raw audio files on phone or any server after the operation.','C1, C2, E2; no disk/log persistence'],['AC-01.3','No match <= 600 s: unchanged route and alternate-place question within 3 s.','C3 result -> C1 speech'],['AC-01.4','Unparseable speech: unchanged route; repeat prompt within 2 s after speech ends.','C2 result/failure -> C1 speech'],['AC-02.1','Mute: mic stops within 1 s; indicator shows muted.','Local C1 <-> E1 control'],['AC-02.2','Mic fails to stop: force-stop within 2 s; 0 post-request audio sent/stored.','Local C1 capture/send gate plus E1 stop']],[1,4.55,1.65])
p('AC-01.1 timing is confirmed: start the 3-second timer at the end of the driver\'s spoken "okay," and stop it when the approved route is active in both the map and turn-by-turn guidance. Approval recognition, validation, any necessary route refresh, and route application are included in this budget. Time spent preparing the proposal or waiting for approval is outside this timer. AC-01.3 retains its 3-second alternate-place prompt deadline after the initial request; AC-01.4 retains its 2-second repeat-prompt deadline after speech ends. Mute timers start at the tap or local recognition of the complete mute command; normal shutdown must finish within 1 second and forced shutdown within 2 seconds of that original mute request. These are acceptance targets to verify during implementation.')
p('Audio lifecycle: capture -> transient phone buffer -> transient backend/provider processing -> disposal. No component writes a raw audio file. Muting discards queued audio and prevents audio captured after the request from leaving the phone; cancellation cannot retract audio already transmitted before mute. Provider logging, retention, crash dumps, and training-use settings must satisfy the no-persistence requirement before E2 is accepted.')
p('Weather semantics: the app evaluates weather information at the route locations and relevant travel times. For US-02, active precipitation is a required context; forecasts support later arrival points when available. Rain avoidance is a preference evaluated by C3 across available candidate routes, not a guarantee that rain is absent. The exact threshold, maximum weather detour, data freshness window, and behavior outside forecast coverage need agreement in D1.')
p('Confirmed approval policy: Veer presents the proposed change and waits for an explicit spoken "okay." Silence is not approval and leaves the active route unchanged. UC-01 step 6 is revised to require explicit approval; step 7 applies the approved route within 3 seconds after that approval utterance ends. A rejection requests another candidate. If approval is unclear, the route stays unchanged and Veer asks for clarification. If the update cannot complete within the deadline, retain the current route and report the failure; such a result does not satisfy the successful-update acceptance criterion. This decision supersedes the original silence-as-approval wording and the earlier confirmation-prompt timing interpretation.')
page();h('6. Architecture pattern and justification')
p('Client-server governs communication between C1 on the phone and a shared backend containing C2-C4. Layered architecture separates live interaction, speech interpretation, route planning, and operational accounting. A pipeline governs speech-to-route information flow: audio -> transcript -> constraint -> route proposal -> confirmed guidance. A local control path governs mute; it does not depend on a cloud response. These are logical components, not independently deployed microservices.')
table(['Class criterion','Fit and justification'],[['Fit to the problem','The pipeline preserves the meaning of a spoken request as it becomes a route constraint. The mobile client owns final route application and listening state; provider modules do not directly control the phone.'],['Team skills','The architecture divides mobile interaction, speech integration, routing, weather integration, and usage accounting across five student owners. It concentrates deployment work in one backend and avoids requiring every member to manage distributed infrastructure. D1 will select languages and tools against the owners\' demonstrated experience.'],['Performance and timing','Approval recognition and route activation must finish within 3 s after spoken "okay" ends. Prepare the proposed route before requesting approval; revalidate or refresh it if needed within the update budget. Failed interpretation must prompt within 2 s. Local mute avoids network latency for 1 s normal and 2 s forced-stop budgets. Candidate-route/weather calls require measurement and bounded deadlines.'],['Scalability','Stateless speech/routing requests can scale across backend instances. Trip IDs and revisions prevent stale updates. Usage limits and provider quotas govern admission; paid usage must not exceed the team\'s configured budget.'],['Hardware constraints','Use the driver\'s existing mounted phone with microphone, speaker, location, and a data connection. Battery, permission handling, cabin noise, background execution, and mobile connectivity constrain the design. No vehicle hardware is required.']],[1.3,5.9])
doc.add_heading('Project constraints',2)
p('Economic: Veer is funded by the team. Prioritize free-tier hosted inference and Google Maps credits for routing and place search. Run on the driver\'s existing phone rather than custom in-dash hardware. The shared backend limits hosting overhead; C4 tracks provider consumption. The team must set actual spending caps and verify credit availability before paid usage.')
p('Security: continuous listening while the vehicle moves makes microphone audio the highest-risk asset. Transcribe each utterance, extract its constraint, then discard audio and transient transcript state. Retain active constraints only for the trip. A visible listening indicator and one-tap passenger/stopped-driver mute are required before any in-car demo. On-device transcription is an alternative to evaluate because it keeps raw audio off third-party servers; hosted transcription remains the current D0 proposal only if its no-retention configuration meets the requirement.')
p('Legal design constraints supplied by the team: driving-time interaction is voice-operated; no feature asks the moving driver to tap the screen. The team cites ORC 4511.204 as the basis for this constraint. Google results must use a compatible Google base map. The team specifies a 30-day upper bound for eligible place-data caching; this does not authorize caching every field. Do not persist provider data by default at D0. Before adding a cache in D1, verify field-specific terms, expire eligible entries within the permitted period, and apply any stricter restriction. Current law and provider terms require verification before implementation; this document does not establish legal compliance.')
p('Ethical: responses use one short sentence; route proposals remain at most 15 words. Longer explanations are shown for a passenger or when stopped. Business selection uses only stated preference and detour cost, with no paid-placement preference. C3 owns selection; C1 owns brief spoken presentation. An unclear request leaves the route unchanged.')
p('Rejected: microservices for the first release, because multiple deployments add latency, monitoring, and coordination overhead under short budgets. A cloud-only mute path was rejected because network failure would block a required local privacy action. Embedded sensor-actuator architecture is not the governing pattern because Veer uses an existing phone rather than dedicated vehicle hardware.')
page();h('7. Decision log')
table(['ID and decision','Alternatives considered','Reason'],[['D01 Use a mobile client plus a layered shared backend.','Web planner; client-only app; microservices.','Matches mounted-phone live navigation and limits deployment overhead. Chosen D0 architecture.'],['D02 Keep microphone control and mute local.','Ask backend/model to decide when to stop capture.','Required for 1 s mute and immediate post-request transmission blocking. Adopted from US-04/UC-02.'],['D03 Use transient speech processing with no audio files.','Store audio for debugging; retain trip conversations.','Meets AC-01.2 and the project discard policy. E2 retention behavior is a provider-selection gate.'],['D04 Represent speech as typed routing constraints.','Send free text directly to routing; let AI invent routes.','Provides validated inputs for waypoint, highway, toll, and weather decisions. Traces to US-01/US-02/US-05.'],['D05 Use Google Maps for routes/places; separate weather input.','Build all map infrastructure; assume Maps supports rain avoidance directly.','Matches the named dependency; C3 combines weather with route candidates. Specific APIs/navigation support need verification.'],['D06 Track provider attempts by trip ID.','Collect raw request logs; rely only on a monthly bill.','Supports US-03 with minimal metadata and counts retries. Aiden owns C4; retention duration remains open.'],['D07 Require explicit spoken confirmation before a route update.','Treat silence as acceptance; automatically apply every parsed request.','Require spoken "okay"; silence leaves the route unchanged. Complete the update within 3 s after "okay" ends. This supersedes UC-01 silence acceptance.'],['D08 Continue existing guidance when new replanning fails.','Apply an uncertain route; stop all navigation on optional failure.','Preserves the active route on no match, parse failure, missing weather, or quota exhaustion. Offline map/routing behavior is not promised.'],['D09 Keep spoken replies brief.','Read detailed explanations aloud; require screen interaction in motion.','One short sentence limits attention demands; longer material is for a passenger or a stop. Required by the ethical constraint.'],['D10 Rank businesses by preference and detour cost.','Sponsored ranking; popularity-only ranking.','Avoid paid steering of driver choices. Required by the ethical constraint.']],[1.7,2.1,3.4])
p('D1 follow-up: select the initial mobile operating system, hosting environment, provider services, and numerical spending caps. Evaluate on-device transcription against the hosted speech contract; define telemetry retention and precipitation/detour parameters. Verify provider navigation/display terms and audio-retention behavior. These implementation choices preserve the D0 component responsibilities and require interface revisions if deployment boundaries change.')
